// Built from tools/qsrc/s1_batch1.py. Append-only.
Q.push(
["Human development", "An 80-year-old man volunteers for a study of healthy aging. He walks 2 miles daily, has no chronic illnesses and takes no medications. He never smoked. Temperature is 36.8°C (98.2°F), pulse is 68/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 138/76 mm Hg. Cardiac and lung examinations show no abnormalities. Pulmonary function tests are performed and compared with results from healthy 25-year-old volunteers. Which of the following is most likely to be found in this man as a normal effect of aging?", ["Increased glomerular filtration rate", "Increased time spent in REM sleep", "Increased residual volume with decreased vital capacity", "Increased lean body mass", "Increased maximal heart rate during exercise"], 2, "With age, lungs lose elastic recoil and the chest wall stiffens, so air is trapped: residual volume rises and vital capacity falls (TLC stays about the same). GFR, maximal heart rate, lean body mass and REM sleep all decrease with age.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Human development", "A mother brings her infant son to the clinic for a well-child examination. He was born at term after an uncomplicated pregnancy. She reports that he now sits steadily without support and recently started crawling across the living room. He babbles \"mama\" and \"dada\" but does not use these words for his parents specifically. He cries and clings to her when his grandparents, whom he sees monthly, try to hold him. During the examination he picks up a small piece of cereal using his thumb and index finger, although somewhat clumsily. Weight and length are at the 50th percentile. Which of the following is the most likely age of this child?", ["9 months", "2 months", "4 months", "24 months", "15 months"], 0, "Stranger anxiety, crawling, nonspecific \"mama/dada\" and an emerging pincer grasp fit 9 months. At 15 months a child walks and uses a few words with meaning; at 24 months a child runs and uses 2-word phrases.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Blood & immune", "A 32-year-old woman comes to the physician because of fatigue and shortness of breath when climbing stairs for 4 months. Her menstrual periods occur every 28 days and last 7 days, and she changes a soaked pad every 2 hours on the heaviest days. She follows a regular diet and takes no medications. She has noticed that she craves ice and chews it throughout the day. Pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Her conjunctivae are pale, and her nails are thin and spoon-shaped. Laboratory studies show:\n\nHemoglobin 9.1 g/dL\nMean corpuscular volume 71 μm3\nRed cell distribution width 17.8% (N = 11.5–14.5)\nFerritin 6 ng/mL\nTotal iron-binding capacity 480 μg/dL (N = 250–400)\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Sideroblastic anemia", "Iron deficiency anemia", "Lead poisoning", "Beta-thalassemia minor", "Anemia of chronic disease"], 1, "Microcytic anemia with low ferritin and high TIBC is iron deficiency, here from menstrual blood loss. Anemia of chronic disease has high ferritin and low TIBC. Thalassemia minor has normal iron studies.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 10-year-old boy is brought to the physician because his mother noticed that his eyes have turned yellow during the past 3 days, which began after a mild viral illness. He has had similar episodes twice before. His father had his spleen removed at age 15 years for \"a blood problem,\" and his paternal aunt had gallstones at age 22 years. Temperature is 37.2°C (99°F). There is scleral icterus, and the spleen tip is palpable 3 cm below the left costal margin. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 9.8 g/dL, reticulocyte count of 9%, increased mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration, and increased indirect bilirubin. A direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is negative. A peripheral blood smear shows many small, round erythrocytes that lack central pallor. Eosin-5-maleimide binding by flow cytometry is decreased. A defect in which of the following proteins is most likely responsible for this patient's condition?", ["Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase", "CD55 and CD59 (GPI-anchored proteins)", "Beta-globin chain", "Pyruvate kinase", "Spectrin or ankyrin"], 4, "Hereditary spherocytosis is usually autosomal dominant and caused by defects in red cell membrane skeleton proteins (ankyrin, spectrin, band 3). Cells lose membrane, become spherocytes and are trapped in the spleen. CD55/CD59 loss causes PNH.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 64-year-old man is hospitalized after an elective total knee replacement. He has hypertension treated with amlodipine. After surgery he receives subcutaneous unfractionated heparin for prophylaxis against venous thromboembolism. His platelet count before surgery was 260,000/mm3. On postoperative day 6, he reports pain and swelling of his left calf. Temperature is 37.1°C (98.8°F), pulse is 92/min, and blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. The left calf is swollen, warm and tender. There is a small area of skin necrosis at one of the heparin injection sites on the abdomen. Today his platelet count is 105,000/mm3. Doppler ultrasonography shows a thrombus in the left popliteal vein. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this patient's thrombocytopenia?", ["IgG antibodies against complexes of heparin and platelet factor 4", "Direct heparin toxicity to megakaryocytes", "Splenic sequestration of platelets", "Heparin inhibition of thrombopoietin", "Consumption of platelets by disseminated intravascular coagulation"], 0, "Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia type 2 appears 5 to 10 days after starting heparin. Antibodies to heparin-PF4 activate platelets, causing thrombocytopenia AND thrombosis. Stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban.", 1, "C", "Immunology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 71-year-old woman with newly diagnosed atrial fibrillation is started on warfarin to reduce her risk of stroke. She also takes metoprolol for rate control and atorvastatin for hyperlipidemia. Her baseline prothrombin time (PT) and partial thromboplastin time are normal. Thirty-six hours after her first dose, her international normalized ratio has risen from 1.0 to 1.8. Her physician explains that despite this early rise, the drug has not yet reached its full antithrombotic effect, and she continues receiving a bridging injectable anticoagulant for several more days. A decrease in which of the following clotting factors is most responsible for the early rise in this patient's PT?", ["Factor II (prothrombin)", "Factor VIII", "Factor IX", "Factor VII", "Factor X"], 3, "Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, reducing factors II, VII, IX and X. Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 6 hours), so it falls first and raises the PT early. Full anticoagulation needs prothrombin to fall, which takes days. Factor VIII is not vitamin K dependent.", 1, "C", "Pharmacology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 10-month-old boy is brought to the physician because of his fourth ear infection in 4 months. He was hospitalized 2 months ago for pneumonia caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae and was treated for Haemophilus influenzae pneumonia 6 weeks before that. He had no infections during his first 6 months of life. His maternal uncle died of overwhelming infection as a child. He is at the 10th percentile for weight. The tonsils are very small and no cervical lymph nodes can be palpated. The left tympanic membrane is bulging and erythematous. Serum concentrations of IgG, IgA and IgM are all markedly decreased. Flow cytometry shows a normal number of CD3+ T cells and almost no CD19+ cells. A mutation in which of the following genes is most likely responsible for this patient's condition?", ["WAS (Wiskott-Aldrich) gene", "Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK)", "CD40 ligand", "NADPH oxidase", "Adenosine deaminase"], 1, "X-linked agammaglobulinemia: BTK defects block B cell maturation, so B cells and all antibody classes are absent. Infections start after about 6 months, when maternal IgG wanes. CD40L defects cause hyper-IgM syndrome; NADPH oxidase defects cause chronic granulomatous disease.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 34-year-old woman undergoes emergency surgery for a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. She has a history of frequent sinus infections, two episodes of giardiasis, and mild asthma treated with an inhaler. She has never received blood products. Because of significant blood loss she is given a unit of packed red blood cells that is ABO and Rh compatible. Within 5 minutes of starting the transfusion, she develops diffuse hives, wheezing and facial swelling. Her blood pressure falls from 118/72 mm Hg to 74/40 mm Hg. Temperature is 37°C (98.6°F). The transfusion is stopped and she is treated with intramuscular epinephrine. A direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is negative, and no hemoglobin is found in her urine. Which of the following underlying conditions most likely explains this reaction?", ["Hereditary angioedema", "Bruton agammaglobulinemia", "Common variable immunodeficiency", "Selective IgA deficiency", "ABO incompatibility"], 3, "IgA-deficient patients can form anti-IgA antibodies and have anaphylaxis to IgA in donor blood. Future transfusions should use washed or IgA-deficient products. It is the most common primary immunodeficiency and often causes sinopulmonary and GI (Giardia) infections.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 55-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue, night sweats, a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss and a feeling of fullness in his left upper abdomen after eating small meals for 3 months. He works as an accountant and does not smoke. Temperature is 37.4°C (99.3°F). The spleen is palpable 10 cm below the left costal margin. There is no lymphadenopathy. Laboratory studies show a leukocyte count of 140,000/mm3 with neutrophils, bands, metamyelocytes and myelocytes, increased basophils and eosinophils, and 2% blasts. Platelet count is 520,000/mm3. Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase is low. Bone marrow cytogenetics show a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22. Which of the following drugs most directly targets the molecular cause of this patient's disease?", ["Rituximab", "Trastuzumab", "Hydroxyurea", "Imatinib", "All-trans retinoic acid"], 3, "Chronic myeloid leukemia is driven by the BCR-ABL fusion gene from the Philadelphia chromosome, which makes a constantly active tyrosine kinase. Imatinib inhibits it. All-trans retinoic acid treats APL with t(15;17).", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 22-year-old college student comes to the student health clinic because of a painless lump on the right side of his neck for 2 months. He also reports intermittent fevers to 38.5°C (101.3°F), drenching night sweats, and itching. He notices that his neck aches after drinking alcohol. He has lost 4 kg (9 lb) without trying. He has no sick contacts. Examination shows a firm, rubbery, nontender 3-cm lymph node in the right supraclavicular area. A chest x-ray shows a mediastinal mass. An excisional lymph node biopsy shows bands of collagen dividing the node into nodules. Within the nodules are scattered large cells with two mirror-image nuclei and prominent eosinophilic nucleoli, surrounded by many lymphocytes, eosinophils and histiocytes. Which of the following markers are the large cells most likely to express?", ["CD19 and CD20 only", "CD10 and BCL-6", "CD34 and TdT", "CD3 and CD4", "CD15 and CD30"], 4, "Reed-Sternberg cells of classic Hodgkin lymphoma are CD15+ and CD30+. Nodular sclerosis is the most common subtype in young adults. TdT and CD34 mark lymphoblasts (ALL).", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 33-year-old woman, gravida 3, para 2, has a vaginal delivery at 39 weeks' gestation after a rapid labor. Five minutes after delivery she becomes short of breath and confused. Pulse is 128/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 76/40 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 82% on room air. Thirty minutes later, blood oozes from her intravenous catheter sites and gums, and there is heavy uterine bleeding despite a firm uterus. Laboratory studies show:\n\nHemoglobin 7.9 g/dL\nPlatelet count 42,000/mm3\nProthrombin time 24 sec (N = 11–15)\nPartial thromboplastin time 68 sec (N = 25–40)\nFibrinogen 70 mg/dL (N = 200–400)\nD-dimer markedly increased\n\nA peripheral blood smear shows fragmented erythrocytes. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "Von Willebrand disease", "Hemolytic uremic syndrome", "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Disseminated intravascular coagulation"], 4, "DIC consumes clotting factors and platelets: PT and PTT rise, fibrinogen falls, and fibrin breakdown (D-dimer) rises. Obstetric complications such as amniotic fluid embolism are classic triggers. TTP and HUS have normal PT and PTT.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 35-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her husband because she has been confused and has had trouble finding words for the past day. She has had fatigue and headaches for 1 week. She takes an oral contraceptive. Temperature is 38.3°C (100.9°F), pulse is 104/min, and blood pressure is 142/88 mm Hg. She is oriented to person only. There are petechiae on her legs and mild scleral icterus. Laboratory studies show:\n\nHemoglobin 8.2 g/dL\nReticulocytes 8%\nPlatelet count 18,000/mm3\nProthrombin time 12 sec (N = 11–15)\nPartial thromboplastin time 31 sec (N = 25–40)\nLactate dehydrogenase 1,250 U/L\nCreatinine 1.9 mg/dL\nHaptoglobin undetectable\n\nA peripheral blood smear shows many schistocytes. A direct antiglobulin test is negative. Which of the following is the most likely underlying mechanism of this patient's condition?", ["Deficient activity of the vWF-cleaving protease ADAMTS13", "Shiga toxin damage to renal endothelium", "Absent glycoprotein Ib on platelets", "Consumption of clotting factors", "Antibodies against platelet GPIIb/IIIa"], 0, "TTP is usually caused by autoantibodies that inhibit ADAMTS13. Uncleaved large vWF multimers cause platelet microthrombi, hemolysis with schistocytes, and organ damage, with normal PT/PTT. Treat urgently with plasma exchange. Antibodies to GPIIb/IIIa cause ITP.", 1, "C", "Pathology"],
["Blood & immune", "A 45-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue and tingling in both feet for 6 months. He recently noticed that he loses his balance in the shower when he closes his eyes. He has followed a strict vegan diet for 10 years and does not take supplements. He drinks one glass of wine weekly. Pulse is 96/min. His tongue is smooth and red. Vibration and position sense are decreased in both feet, and Romberg sign is present. Ankle reflexes are decreased. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 9.4 g/dL and a mean corpuscular volume of 115 μm3. A blood smear shows macro-ovalocytes and neutrophils with 6 nuclear lobes. Serum methylmalonic acid and homocysteine concentrations are both increased. A deficiency of which of the following vitamins is the most likely cause of this patient's findings?", ["Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)", "Folate (vitamin B9)", "Thiamine (vitamin B1)", "Niacin (vitamin B3)", "Cobalamin (vitamin B12)"], 4, "B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase (homocysteine to methionine) and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Raised methylmalonic acid and neurologic signs (dorsal column damage) separate B12 from folate deficiency, which raises homocysteine only.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 68-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of a tremor in his right hand for 1 year. She says he has become slower in getting dressed, his handwriting has become smaller, and his face looks less expressive. He has had constipation and has lost his sense of smell. He is a retired farmer and has hypertension treated with lisinopril. Examination shows a 4- to 6-Hz tremor of the right hand that is most obvious when his hand is resting in his lap and lessens when he reaches for a cup. There is ratchet-like resistance to passive movement of the right wrist. He walks with short, shuffling steps and reduced arm swing, and turns slowly. Loss of which of the following neurons is most responsible for this patient's motor findings?", ["Cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert", "Motor neurons of the anterior horn", "Purkinje cells of the cerebellum", "GABAergic neurons of the caudate nucleus", "Dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta"], 4, "Parkinson disease destroys dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta; surviving neurons contain Lewy bodies (alpha-synuclein). Caudate loss is Huntington disease; nucleus basalis loss is seen in Alzheimer disease.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 30-year-old woman comes to the physician because of drooping of her eyelids and double vision for 2 months. The symptoms are mild in the morning but worsen by the evening, especially after she works on her computer. Recently she has had difficulty chewing meat at dinner and her voice becomes nasal when she talks for a long time. Vital signs are within normal limits. When she looks upward for 60 seconds, her right eyelid gradually droops. Pupillary responses are normal. Deep tendon reflexes and sensation are normal. Placing an ice pack over her eyes for 2 minutes improves the ptosis. A CT scan of the chest shows an anterior mediastinal mass. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's weakness?", ["Loss of upper motor neurons", "Autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels", "Inhibition of acetylcholine release by a bacterial toxin", "Autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors", "Demyelination of peripheral nerves"], 3, "Myasthenia gravis is a type II hypersensitivity against the nicotinic ACh receptor; it is linked to thymoma and thymic hyperplasia. Antibodies to presynaptic calcium channels cause Lambert-Eaton syndrome, where strength improves with repeated use.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 52-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by police after he was found wandering confused outside a shelter. He has a long history of heavy alcohol use and was treated for alcohol withdrawal 6 months ago. He appears thin and disheveled. Temperature is 36.6°C (97.9°F), pulse is 102/min, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. He is disoriented to place and time. There is horizontal nystagmus and he cannot move either eye outward. He has a wide-based, unsteady gait. Fingerstick glucose is 72 mg/dL. The emergency physician gives an intravenous vitamin before any glucose-containing fluids. The deficient vitamin in this patient is a cofactor for which of the following enzymes?", ["Glutamate decarboxylase", "Lysyl oxidase", "Pyruvate dehydrogenase", "Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase", "Pyruvate carboxylase"], 2, "Wernicke encephalopathy is thiamine (B1) deficiency. Thiamine pyrophosphate is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase and branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase. Give thiamine before glucose. Biotin serves pyruvate carboxylase; B12 serves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A right-handed 70-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after her husband noticed that her speech suddenly became slow and effortful while they were eating breakfast. She has hypertension, type 2 diabetes and atrial fibrillation, and she stopped taking her anticoagulant 2 weeks ago because of the cost. Blood pressure is 174/96 mm Hg and pulse is 104/min and irregularly irregular. She follows commands correctly but speaks in short phrases of 2 or 3 words, and she is frustrated by her difficulty. There is drooping of the lower right face. Strength is 2/5 in the right arm and 4/5 in the right leg. Sensation is mildly decreased on the right arm. A noncontrast CT scan of the head shows no hemorrhage. Occlusion of which of the following arteries is most likely?", ["Left posterior cerebral artery", "Basilar artery", "Left anterior cerebral artery", "Right middle cerebral artery", "Left middle cerebral artery"], 4, "Face and arm weakness greater than leg points to the lateral motor cortex, supplied by the MCA. Expressive (Broca) aphasia means the dominant left hemisphere. The ACA supplies the medial cortex, so its stroke affects the leg most.", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 26-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after he was stabbed in the back with a knife during a fight. He is alert and oriented. Pulse is 98/min and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. There is a 2-cm wound just to the left of the midline at the level of the T10 vertebra. Neurologic examination of the upper extremities is normal. An MRI shows that the left half of the spinal cord at the T10 level has been cut, and the right half is intact. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely on neurologic examination of this patient's lower extremities?", ["Left-sided loss of all sensation with normal strength", "Loss of pain and temperature on both sides only", "Left leg weakness and loss of vibration sense; right-sided loss of pain and temperature", "Bilateral leg weakness with loss of all sensation", "Right leg weakness and loss of vibration sense; left-sided loss of pain and temperature"], 2, "Brown-Sequard syndrome: corticospinal tract and dorsal columns cross in the brainstem, so their loss is on the same side. The spinothalamic tract crosses near its entry level, so pain and temperature are lost on the opposite side.", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 39-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with a diabetic foot infection caused by methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. She has major depressive disorder that has been well controlled with fluoxetine for 3 years. Two days after she begins receiving intravenous linezolid, she becomes restless and anxious and has diarrhea. Temperature is 39.4°C (102.9°F), pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 168/98 mm Hg. She is diaphoretic and her pupils are dilated. Deep tendon reflexes are 4+ in the lower extremities, with sustained clonus at the ankles and spontaneous jerks of the legs. There is no muscle rigidity. Which of the following drugs is the most appropriate specific treatment for this patient's condition?", ["Cyproheptadine", "Bromocriptine", "Physostigmine", "Dantrolene", "Naloxone"], 0, "This is serotonin syndrome; linezolid is a weak MAO inhibitor. Cyproheptadine is a 5-HT2A antagonist. Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia; bromocriptine treats neuroleptic malignant syndrome, which has rigidity rather than clonus.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 34-year-old woman comes to the physician because she has been urinating every 1 to 2 hours, day and night, for 3 months, and she is always thirsty. She has bipolar I disorder; she has had no manic or depressive episodes since starting a mood stabilizer 2 years ago, and she has had a fine tremor of her hands since then. She takes no other medications. Blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Her serum sodium is 146 mEq/L and fasting glucose is 88 mg/dL. Her 24-hour urine volume is 6 L. During a water deprivation test, urine osmolality remains 180 mOsm/kg. After she receives desmopressin, urine osmolality rises only to 195 mOsm/kg. Which of the following medications most likely caused this patient's condition?", ["Carbamazepine", "Valproate", "Quetiapine", "Lithium", "Lamotrigine"], 3, "Lithium causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus by reducing collecting duct response to ADH, so desmopressin does not help. Other lithium effects: hypothyroidism, tremor, and Ebstein anomaly if taken in pregnancy. Carbamazepine can cause SIADH, the opposite problem.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 22-year-old man is brought to the physician by his parents because of strange behavior for 3 months. He dropped out of his university classes and spends most of the day in his room. He says that the neighbors have placed cameras in the walls to spy on him, and he hears two men's voices talking to each other about what he is doing. He has not showered for a week and speaks little. He has never had a period of elevated mood, reduced need for sleep or persistent sadness. He does not use alcohol or drugs, and a urine toxicology screen is negative. Physical examination, thyroid studies and an MRI of the brain show no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Schizophrenia", "Schizoaffective disorder", "Brief psychotic disorder", "Delusional disorder", "Schizophreniform disorder"], 4, "The diagnosis depends on duration: brief psychotic disorder lasts under 1 month, schizophreniform disorder 1 to 6 months, and schizophrenia more than 6 months. Delusional disorder has delusions without hallucinations or other psychotic features.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 50-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of the worst headache of her life, which began suddenly 3 hours ago, and drooping of her right eyelid. She has hypertension and smokes one pack of cigarettes daily. Blood pressure is 172/98 mm Hg. Her neck is stiff. The right upper eyelid covers most of the pupil. When the lid is lifted, the right eye is positioned down and out, and she cannot move it up, down or inward. The right pupil is 7 mm and does not constrict to light; the left pupil is 3 mm and reacts normally. The remainder of the neurologic examination shows no abnormalities. Compression of which of the following structures is the most likely cause of this patient's eye findings?", ["Right abducens nerve by raised intracranial pressure", "Right sympathetic chain by a lung tumor", "Right oculomotor nerve by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm", "Right optic nerve by a pituitary tumor", "Right trochlear nerve in the cavernous sinus"], 2, "Parasympathetic pupil fibers run on the outside of CN III, so compression (classically a PComm aneurysm) dilates the pupil early. Diabetic CN III palsy affects the center of the nerve and usually spares the pupil. Sympathetic loss causes a small pupil (Horner).", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 35-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by his wife because of fever, severe headache, and confusion for 12 hours. Two weeks ago he had an ear infection that he did not treat. He has no history of serious illness and is not taking any medications. Temperature is 39.6°C (103.3°F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. He is drowsy but arousable. Flexion of his neck causes flexion of his hips and knees. There is no rash. A lumbar puncture shows an opening pressure of 34 cm H2O. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis shows 2,000 leukocytes/mm3 (92% neutrophils), glucose of 18 mg/dL, and protein of 290 mg/dL. A Gram stain shows lancet-shaped, gram-positive cocci in pairs. Which of the following is the most likely causal organism?", ["Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Streptococcus agalactiae", "Haemophilus influenzae type b", "Listeria monocytogenes", "Neisseria meningitidis"], 0, "Lancet-shaped gram-positive diplococci are S. pneumoniae, the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in adults. N. meningitidis is a gram-negative diplococcus; Listeria is a gram-positive rod, important in neonates, the elderly and the immunocompromised.", 1, "F", "Microbiology"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 40-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of personality changes and abnormal movements for 1 year. She says he has become irritable and impulsive, lost his job as an electrician because of mistakes, and constantly fidgets. His father had similar symptoms beginning at age 50 years and died in a nursing home; his paternal grandmother was described as \"nervous\" and died in a psychiatric hospital. Examination shows brief, irregular, flowing movements of his face, hands and trunk that he cannot suppress. He cannot keep his tongue protruded for 10 seconds. He has difficulty with serial sevens. An MRI of the brain shows atrophy of the caudate nuclei with enlarged frontal horns of the lateral ventricles. Which of the following genetic findings is most likely in this patient?", ["Deletion in the SMN1 gene", "Expanded CGG repeats in the FMR1 gene", "Expanded CAG trinucleotide repeats in the HTT gene", "A point mutation in the APP gene", "Expanded GAA repeats in the frataxin gene"], 2, "Huntington disease is autosomal dominant with CAG repeat expansion. Repeats can grow when passed down, especially from the father, so symptoms start earlier in later generations (anticipation). CGG is fragile X; GAA is Friedreich ataxia.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Nervous system & behavior", "A 76-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her daughter because of gradually worsening memory over 2 years. She repeats the same questions during conversations, has missed paying several bills, and last week she got lost driving to a grocery store she has used for 20 years. She has hypertension treated with lisinopril. Her vital signs are normal. She is cooperative but scores 19/30 on the Mini-Mental State Examination, with the greatest difficulty in recall. Neurologic examination shows no focal findings and her gait is normal. Serum vitamin B12 and thyroid-stimulating hormone levels are normal. An MRI of the brain shows atrophy of the hippocampi and parietal lobes. Her physician starts donepezil. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of this drug?", ["Removes amyloid plaques from the brain", "Inhibits monoamine oxidase B", "Increases dopamine release", "Blocks NMDA glutamate receptors", "Inhibits acetylcholinesterase in the brain"], 4, "Alzheimer disease reduces cholinergic neurons (nucleus basalis of Meynert). Donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine inhibit acetylcholinesterase to raise ACh. Memantine is the NMDA antagonist used in moderate to severe disease.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 5-year-old boy is brought to the physician because he falls often and has trouble keeping up with other children at school. He began walking at 18 months of age. His maternal uncle used a wheelchair from age 11 years and died at age 19 years of heart failure. Examination shows a waddling gait and enlarged, firm calves. When asked to stand up from the floor, he rolls onto his hands and knees and pushes on his thighs with his hands to rise. Serum creatine kinase is 18,000 U/L (N = 25–200). A muscle biopsy shows variation in fiber size, necrotic fibers, and replacement of muscle by fat and connective tissue. Immunostaining shows no dystrophin. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?", ["A deletion in the SMN1 gene", "A nonframeshift deletion in the dystrophin gene", "CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene", "A frameshift deletion in the dystrophin gene", "Antibodies against muscle acetylcholine receptors"], 3, "Duchenne muscular dystrophy is X-linked; frameshift deletions make no functional dystrophin, so muscle fibers break down and are replaced by fat (pseudohypertrophy). Nonframeshift deletions cause the milder Becker form. CTG repeats cause myotonic dystrophy.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 4-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department after she fell from a low step and now refuses to bear weight on her right leg. X-rays show a fracture of the right femur. She has had four other fractures, of the forearm, clavicle and ribs, after minor falls. Child protective services investigated after the second fracture and found no evidence of abuse. Her father is 152 cm (5 ft) tall, wears hearing aids, and had many fractures as a child. Examination shows bluish-gray sclerae and discolored, translucent teeth. Her joints are hypermobile. X-rays show thin bones with decreased density and several healed fractures. Defective synthesis of which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?", ["Type I collagen", "Type III collagen", "Type IV collagen", "Type II collagen", "Fibrillin-1"], 0, "Osteogenesis imperfecta is usually an autosomal dominant defect in COL1A1 or COL1A2. Type I collagen is in bone, sclera, teeth, tendon and the middle ear bones. Type IV defects cause Alport syndrome; fibrillin defects cause Marfan syndrome.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 55-year-old man comes to the physician for a follow-up visit after his third episode in 1 year of sudden, severe pain and swelling of his right big toe, which began overnight after a large meal with beer. Each episode resolved within a week with naproxen. He has hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide. His BMI is 33 kg/m2. Examination shows a firm, painless nodule on the rim of his left ear. Serum uric acid concentration is 9.6 mg/dL. Analysis of joint fluid from the last episode showed needle-shaped crystals that were negatively birefringent under polarized light. To prevent future episodes, he is started on a drug that lowers uric acid production. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug?", ["Inhibits xanthine oxidase", "Inhibits microtubule polymerization in neutrophils", "Increases renal uric acid excretion", "Inhibits cyclooxygenase", "Converts uric acid to allantoin"], 0, "Allopurinol and febuxostat inhibit xanthine oxidase, lowering uric acid production. Colchicine blocks microtubules (acute attacks), probenecid increases excretion, and pegloticase converts uric acid to allantoin.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 3.9-kg (8.6-lb) newborn boy is examined in the nursery. He was delivered vaginally at 41 weeks' gestation to a mother with gestational diabetes. Delivery was complicated by shoulder dystocia, and the obstetrician applied strong downward traction on the head. Examination shows that the right arm hangs at his side, rotated inward, with the elbow extended, the forearm pronated and the wrist flexed. The Moro reflex is absent on the right side. He grasps the examiner's finger firmly with both hands. A clavicle x-ray shows no fracture. Injury to which of the following parts of the brachial plexus is most likely?", ["Radial nerve at the spiral groove", "Posterior cord", "Lower trunk (C8 and T1)", "Upper trunk (C5 and C6)", "Long thoracic nerve"], 3, "Erb palsy (\"waiter's tip\") comes from stretching the upper trunk when the head is pulled away from the shoulder. Abductors, lateral rotators and biceps lose function. Lower trunk injury (Klumpke) causes a claw hand.", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 45-year-old woman comes to the physician because of pain and stiffness in both hands for 4 months. Her hands are stiff for about 2 hours after she wakes up and improve as she uses them. She also reports fatigue and has had difficulty opening jars. Her mother had hypothyroidism. Temperature is 37.4°C (99.3°F). There is warm, tender swelling of the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints and the proximal interphalangeal joints of both hands, and of both wrists. The distal interphalangeal joints are normal. Hand x-rays show soft tissue swelling and periarticular osteopenia. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 48 mm/h. Which of the following antibodies is most specific for this patient's most likely diagnosis?", ["Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody", "Antinuclear antibody", "Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibody", "Rheumatoid factor", "Anti-centromere antibody"], 2, "Rheumatoid arthritis is linked to HLA-DR4. Anti-CCP is the most specific test; rheumatoid factor (IgM against IgG Fc) is more often positive but less specific. Anti-dsDNA is specific for lupus; anti-centromere suggests limited systemic sclerosis.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 60-year-old man comes to the physician because of joint pain in his hands and knees, fatigue and sharp chest pain when he breathes deeply for 1 month. He has hypertension, which has been treated for 1 year with hydralazine and metoprolol. He has no rash. Temperature is 37.8°C (100°F). There are decreased breath sounds at the left lung base. A chest x-ray shows a small left pleural effusion. Urinalysis is normal, and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL. Antinuclear antibody testing is positive at 1:640 in a homogeneous pattern. Complement levels are normal. Which of the following antibodies is most likely to be present in this patient?", ["Anti-Jo-1 antibody", "Anti-histone antibody", "Anti-Scl-70 antibody", "Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody", "Anti-Smith antibody"], 1, "Drug-induced lupus (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid) gives anti-histone antibodies and rarely involves the kidneys or brain. It improves after the drug is stopped. Anti-Smith and anti-dsDNA point to systemic lupus.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 30-year-old man comes to the physician because of itchy skin lesions on his elbows, knees and scalp for 6 months. The lesions became worse after a stressful job change. Two new lesions appeared on his forearm at the site of a cat scratch. His father has \"dry skin patches\" and arthritis of the fingers. Examination shows sharply demarcated, salmon-pink plaques covered with silvery-white scale on the extensor surfaces of the elbows and knees and along the hairline. Removing the scale causes small points of bleeding. Several fingernails have small pits and yellow-brown discoloration. A biopsy of one plaque is performed. Which of the following histologic findings is most likely?", ["Parakeratosis with neutrophil collections in the stratum corneum", "Subepidermal blisters with IgG along the basement membrane", "Intercellular edema of the epidermis (spongiosis)", "Acantholysis with intraepidermal blisters", "Noncaseating granulomas in the dermis"], 0, "Psoriasis shows acanthosis, parakeratosis (retained nuclei in the stratum corneum) and Munro microabscesses. Acantholysis is pemphigus vulgaris; linear basement membrane IgG with subepidermal blisters is bullous pemphigoid; spongiosis is eczema.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 50-year-old woman comes to the physician because a mole on her upper back has grown and changed color over 6 months. She has fair skin, red hair, and had several blistering sunburns as a child. She works outdoors as a landscaper. Examination shows a 9-mm lesion with irregular, notched borders and areas of black, brown and pink; a small part of the lesion is raised. There are no palpable axillary or cervical lymph nodes. An excisional biopsy confirms the diagnosis of melanoma with a vertical growth phase. Which of the following features of the lesion is most important for predicting the risk of metastasis?", ["Patient's skin type", "Diameter of the lesion", "Number of nevi on the body", "Degree of pigmentation", "Depth of invasion (Breslow thickness)"], 4, "Melanoma's risk of spread depends mostly on how deep it grows into the dermis (vertical growth phase). The ABCDE features help find melanoma but do not predict prognosis as well as depth.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 29-year-old woman at 32 weeks' gestation comes to the physician because of numbness and tingling in her right hand for 3 weeks. The symptoms often wake her at night, and she shakes her hand to relieve them. She works as a medical transcriptionist. Her pregnancy has otherwise been uncomplicated, but she has gained weight and has swelling of both ankles. Examination shows decreased sensation over the palmar surface of the thumb, index finger, middle finger and half of the ring finger. Sensation over the thenar eminence is normal. Thumb opposition strength is 4/5. Tapping over the palmar wrist reproduces the tingling, as does holding both wrists fully flexed for 60 seconds. Compression of which of the following nerves is most likely?", ["Median nerve", "Radial nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve", "Anterior interosseous nerve", "Ulnar nerve"], 0, "Carpal tunnel syndrome compresses the median nerve under the flexor retinaculum. It is linked to pregnancy, hypothyroidism, diabetes and repetitive use. Sensation over the thenar eminence is spared because the palmar cutaneous branch runs above the tunnel.", 1, "F", "Anatomy"],
["Musculoskeletal & skin", "A 68-year-old woman comes for a follow-up visit after a fall in which she fractured her left wrist. She went through menopause at age 49 years and never took hormone therapy. She smokes half a pack of cigarettes daily and drinks two glasses of wine nightly. She has lost 4 cm (1.6 in) of height over 10 years. Serum calcium, phosphate, 25-hydroxyvitamin D and parathyroid hormone concentrations are within the reference ranges. Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry shows a T-score of –3.0 at the lumbar spine. She is started on alendronate and instructed to take it in the morning with a full glass of water and to remain upright for at least 30 minutes afterward. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug?", ["It increases intestinal calcium absorption", "It blocks RANKL as a monoclonal antibody", "It stimulates osteoblasts as a parathyroid hormone analog", "It binds bone mineral and inhibits osteoclast activity", "It activates estrogen receptors in bone"], 3, "Bisphosphonates are pyrophosphate analogs that bind hydroxyapatite and cause osteoclast apoptosis. Staying upright prevents pill esophagitis. Teriparatide is the PTH analog; denosumab blocks RANKL; raloxifene is a SERM.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 66-year-old man is admitted to the coronary care unit 5 days after the onset of chest pain that he ignored at home. He has hypertension and smokes 1 pack of cigarettes daily. An ECG on admission showed Q waves and ST-segment elevations in leads V1 through V4. He was hemodynamically stable and pain free until this morning, when he suddenly became short of breath and lost consciousness briefly. Pulse is 124/min and blood pressure is 70/50 mm Hg; systolic pressure falls by 18 mm Hg during inspiration. Jugular veins are distended to the angle of the mandible. Heart sounds are faint. The lungs are clear. An ECG shows sinus tachycardia with low-voltage QRS complexes. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's current condition?", ["Rupture of the left ventricular free wall", "Dressler syndrome", "Papillary muscle rupture", "Fibrinous pericarditis", "Ventricular aneurysm"], 0, "From about day 3 to day 14, macrophages remove dead tissue and the wall is weakest, so rupture is most likely. Free wall rupture causes cardiac tamponade (Beck triad). Papillary muscle rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation; Dressler syndrome appears weeks later.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 78-year-old man comes to the physician because of chest tightness when he walks uphill and an episode of fainting while climbing stairs 2 weeks ago. He has hypertension and hyperlipidemia, and he had a normal echocardiogram 15 years ago. He has no history of rheumatic fever. Pulse is 76/min and blood pressure is 128/82 mm Hg. The carotid pulses are weak and rise slowly. A grade 4/6 harsh, late-peaking systolic murmur is heard best at the right upper sternal border and radiates to both carotid arteries. The second heart sound is soft. An S4 is present. The lungs are clear. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's murmur?", ["Infective endocarditis of the tricuspid valve", "Myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve", "Age-related calcification of a trileaflet aortic valve", "Dilation of the aortic root", "Rheumatic fusion of the mitral commissures"], 2, "Aortic stenosis in the elderly is usually from wear-and-tear calcification; in younger patients a bicuspid valve calcifies earlier. Angina, syncope and heart failure are the classic symptoms and signal the need for valve replacement.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes is started on atorvastatin after his fasting lipid panel shows an LDL cholesterol of 182 mg/dL. He also takes metformin. Six weeks later he comes to the physician because of aching and weakness in his thighs and shoulders that makes it hard to climb stairs. He has not changed his exercise habits. Recently he started drinking grapefruit juice each morning. Vital signs are normal. His proximal muscles are tender and strength is 4/5 in the hip flexors. Urine is light yellow. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of the most likely cause of his symptoms, and which laboratory test should be checked next?", ["Inhibits HMG-CoA reductase; check creatine kinase", "Inhibits intestinal cholesterol absorption; check liver enzymes", "Inhibits PCSK9; check LDL receptors", "Binds bile acids in the gut; check vitamin K levels", "Activates PPAR-alpha; check triglycerides"], 0, "Statins block the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis, so liver cells make more LDL receptors and clear more LDL. Myopathy is a key adverse effect; check CK. Risk rises with fibrates and CYP3A4 inhibitors.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because his parents notice that he stops and squats during play whenever he becomes short of breath and his lips turn blue. Once, after a long crying episode, he became very blue and limp for a minute. He was born at term after an uncomplicated pregnancy. He is at the 10th percentile for weight. Oxygen saturation is 82% on room air. His fingers are clubbed. A harsh systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border, and the second heart sound is single. A chest x-ray shows a boot-shaped heart with decreased pulmonary vascular markings. Which of the following best explains how squatting relieves this patient's symptoms?", ["It relieves the pulmonary stenosis", "It raises systemic vascular resistance and decreases the right-to-left shunt", "It lowers pulmonary vascular resistance", "It decreases venous return to the heart", "It closes the ventricular septal defect"], 1, "Squatting kinks the femoral arteries and raises systemic resistance, so more right ventricular blood goes to the lungs instead of across the VSD. Tetralogy comes from anterosuperior displacement of the infundibular septum.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 3-year-old boy of Japanese descent is brought to the physician because of fever for 6 days despite acetaminophen. His mother says he has been very irritable. Temperature is 39.8°C (103.6°F). Both eyes are red without discharge. His lips are red, dry and cracked, and his tongue is bright red with prominent papillae. There is a diffuse red rash on his trunk. His hands and feet are swollen and red. There is a single 2-cm nontender lymph node on the left side of his neck. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are increased, and platelet count is 640,000/mm3. Throat culture is negative. Without appropriate treatment, this patient is at greatest risk of developing which of the following complications?", ["Aortic dissection", "Mitral stenosis", "Renal artery stenosis", "Pulmonary hypertension", "Coronary artery aneurysm"], 4, "Kawasaki disease is a medium-vessel vasculitis of young children. Treat with IVIG and aspirin to lower the risk of coronary aneurysm, thrombosis and myocardial infarction. It is one of the few times aspirin is given to a child.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 10-year-old boy from a crowded area is brought to the physician because of fever and painful, swollen joints for 1 week. His left knee was swollen first, then his right ankle, then his left wrist. Three weeks ago he had a sore throat that was not treated. Temperature is 38.6°C (101.5°F) and pulse is 118/min. The left wrist is red, warm and tender. There are painless, firm nodules over his elbows and a pink rash with ring-shaped borders on his trunk. A soft holosystolic murmur is heard at the apex and radiates to the axilla. Antistreptolysin O titer is increased. An ECG shows a prolonged PR interval. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this patient's heart damage?", ["Exotoxin-mediated destruction of myocytes", "Immune complexes deposited in the endocardium", "T-cell response to a viral superantigen", "Direct bacterial invasion of the myocardium", "Antibodies against streptococcal M protein cross-react with cardiac proteins"], 4, "Acute rheumatic fever is a type II hypersensitivity caused by molecular mimicry. The mitral valve is most often affected; chronic disease causes mitral stenosis. Aschoff bodies are characteristic.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 52-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a dry, tickling cough for 6 weeks. The cough keeps her awake at night but she has no fever, shortness of breath, heartburn or nasal symptoms. Three months ago she was diagnosed with hypertension and type 2 diabetes, and she started lisinopril and metformin at that time. She has never smoked. Blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Lung examination and a chest x-ray show no abnormalities. Her physician plans to replace one of her medications with losartan. Which of the following best explains this patient's cough?", ["Increased bradykinin levels", "Reduced angiotensin II levels in the lung", "Direct bronchospasm from beta receptor blockade", "Increased aldosterone levels", "Increased histamine release from mast cells"], 0, "ACE also breaks down bradykinin. ACE inhibitors raise bradykinin, causing cough and sometimes angioedema. An angiotensin receptor blocker such as losartan is a good substitute because it does not affect bradykinin.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 28-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of fever, chills and pleuritic chest pain for 5 days. He injects heroin daily and has shared needles. Temperature is 39.5°C (103.1°F), pulse is 116/min, and blood pressure is 108/64 mm Hg. There are needle track marks on both forearms. A grade 3/6 holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border; it becomes louder during inspiration. Jugular venous pulsations show large v waves. A chest x-ray shows several round, cavitating nodules in both lungs. Three sets of blood cultures are drawn. Which of the following organisms and heart valves are most likely involved?", ["Staphylococcus epidermidis infecting the aortic valve", "Streptococcus gallolyticus infecting the mitral valve", "Enterococcus infecting the pulmonic valve", "Staphylococcus aureus infecting the tricuspid valve", "Viridans streptococci infecting the mitral valve"], 3, "Injection drug use most often causes S. aureus endocarditis of the tricuspid valve; right-sided murmurs get louder with inspiration. Viridans streptococci affect damaged valves after dental work; S. epidermidis affects prosthetic valves.", 1, "F", "Microbiology"],
["Cardiovascular", "A 17-year-old boy is brought to the physician for a preparticipation sports examination before joining his high school basketball team. He has had two episodes of light-headedness while running sprints. His paternal uncle died suddenly while playing soccer at age 24 years. Pulse is 64/min and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. A grade 3/6 harsh systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border; it does not radiate to the carotids. An S4 is present. An ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy with deep, narrow Q waves in the lateral leads. Echocardiography shows asymmetric thickening of the interventricular septum. Which of the following maneuvers would most likely increase the intensity of this patient's murmur?", ["Squatting", "Valsalva maneuver", "Sustained hand grip", "Lying down from standing", "Passive leg raise"], 1, "In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy the outflow obstruction worsens when the LV is smaller. Valsalva and standing reduce preload, so the murmur gets louder. Squatting, leg raise and hand grip increase preload or afterload and soften it. Most cases are autosomal dominant sarcomere gene mutations.", 1, "C", "Physiology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 38-year-old woman comes to the physician because of shortness of breath with exertion that has worsened over 2 years. She has never smoked and has no pets. Her brother was diagnosed with \"early emphysema\" at age 42 years. Respirations are 20/min. Breath sounds are decreased at both lung bases, and the expiratory phase is prolonged. The liver edge is firm and nodular. Spirometry shows an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.55 with no improvement after a bronchodilator; total lung capacity is increased and diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide is decreased. A CT scan of the chest shows emphysema involving entire acini, most severe in the lower lobes. A liver biopsy shows PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules in hepatocytes. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?", ["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency", "Cystic fibrosis", "Bronchiectasis from Kartagener syndrome", "Asthma", "Chronic bronchitis"], 0, "Too little alpha-1 antitrypsin leaves elastase unopposed, destroying alveolar walls throughout the acinus, worst in the lower lobes. Misfolded protein builds up in liver cells (PAS-positive globules) and causes cirrhosis. Smoking causes centriacinar emphysema in the upper lobes.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 45-year-old woman is in the hospital 3 days after a total hip replacement. She has not walked since surgery because of pain. This morning she suddenly develops shortness of breath and sharp chest pain on the right that worsens with breathing. She takes an oral estrogen for menopausal symptoms. Temperature is 37.5°C (99.5°F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 88% on room air. The lungs are clear. Her left calf is swollen. A chest x-ray shows no abnormalities. Which of the following sets of arterial blood gas findings is most likely in this patient?", ["High PaO2, high PaCO2, low pH and increased A-a gradient", "Normal PaO2, low PaCO2, high pH and normal A-a gradient", "Low PaO2, low PaCO2, high pH and increased A-a gradient", "Low PaO2, normal PaCO2, normal pH and normal A-a gradient", "Low PaO2, high PaCO2, low pH and normal A-a gradient"], 2, "Pulmonary embolism causes V/Q mismatch, so oxygen falls and the A-a gradient rises. The patient breathes fast and blows off CO2, giving a respiratory alkalosis. A normal A-a gradient with high CO2 suggests hypoventilation.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 35-year-old African American woman comes to the physician because of a dry cough, fatigue and mild shortness of breath for 3 months. She has also had painful red nodules on her shins. She works as a teacher and has never smoked. Temperature is 37.6°C (99.7°F). There are tender, red nodules on both shins. Lung examination shows no abnormalities. A chest x-ray shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Laboratory studies show a serum calcium of 11.8 mg/dL, a low parathyroid hormone concentration, a high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D concentration, and an increased serum angiotensin-converting enzyme activity. A transbronchial biopsy shows tight clusters of epithelioid histiocytes and multinucleated giant cells without central necrosis. Acid-fast and fungal stains are negative. Which of the following best explains this patient's hypercalcemia?", ["Parathyroid hormone-related peptide from the tumor", "Increased 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in granuloma macrophages", "Reduced renal calcium excretion caused by thiazides", "Osteolytic bone lesions", "Increased parathyroid hormone secretion"], 1, "In sarcoidosis, activated macrophages convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, increasing gut calcium absorption. Serum ACE is often elevated. PTHrP causes hypercalcemia in squamous cell carcinoma.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 42-year-old man who emigrated from the Philippines 2 years ago comes to the physician because of a productive cough, fever, night sweats and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss over 2 months. He drinks 4 beers daily. Temperature is 38.1°C (100.6°F). There are crackles over the right upper lung. A chest x-ray shows a cavity in the right upper lobe. Sputum shows acid-fast bacilli, and an HIV test is negative. He is started on a four-drug regimen that includes isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol. Which of the following should also be given to prevent a common adverse effect of this treatment, peripheral neuropathy?", ["Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)", "Thiamine (vitamin B1)", "Folic acid", "Vitamin D", "Cobalamin (vitamin B12)"], 0, "Isoniazid inhibits mycolic acid synthesis and depletes vitamin B6, causing neuropathy. Other effects include hepatotoxicity and drug-induced lupus. Rifampin turns body fluids orange; ethambutol can cause optic neuritis.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 62-year-old man comes to the physician because of a cough for 2 months and confusion and headache for 1 week. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 50 years. He has lost 7 kg (15 lb) without trying. Pulse is 82/min and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. He is euvolemic, without edema or orthostatic changes. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 124 mEq/L, serum osmolality of 258 mOsm/kg, and urine osmolality of 560 mOsm/kg. Thyroid and adrenal function are normal. A CT scan of the chest shows a large mass near the right hilum with bulky mediastinal lymph nodes. A biopsy shows small, round blue cells with scant cytoplasm and crush artifact that stain positive for chromogranin. Which of the following is the most likely type of tumor?", ["Small cell carcinoma", "Adenocarcinoma", "Bronchial carcinoid", "Squamous cell carcinoma", "Large cell carcinoma"], 0, "Small cell lung cancer is central, strongly tied to smoking and arises from neuroendocrine cells. It can secrete ADH (SIADH) or ACTH, and cause Lambert-Eaton syndrome. Squamous cell carcinoma is also central but causes hypercalcemia through PTHrP.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A male newborn is delivered by cesarean section at 30 weeks' gestation because of preterm labor. His mother has type 1 diabetes with poorly controlled glucose during the pregnancy. She did not receive corticosteroids before delivery. Apgar scores are 6 and 7 at 1 and 5 minutes. Within 1 hour of birth he has grunting, nasal flaring and intercostal retractions. Respirations are 72/min, and oxygen saturation is 84% on room air. A chest x-ray shows diffuse fine granular opacities with air bronchograms and low lung volumes. Deficiency of a substance produced by which of the following cells is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?", ["Type I pneumocytes", "Goblet cells", "Club cells", "Alveolar macrophages", "Type II pneumocytes"], 4, "Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome is caused by too little surfactant (mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine), made by type II pneumocytes from about week 26. High fetal insulin in maternal diabetes delays surfactant production. Type I pneumocytes form the gas exchange surface.", 1, "F", "Histology & cell biology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 4-year-old boy is brought to the physician because his mother noticed that his eyelids have been puffy for 1 week and his legs became swollen 3 days ago. Two weeks ago he had an upper respiratory tract infection. Blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. There is swelling around both eyes and pitting edema of both legs and the scrotum. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no blood or casts. Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL and serum cholesterol is 340 mg/dL. Serum creatinine and complement levels are normal. Light microscopy of a kidney biopsy shows normal glomeruli, and immunofluorescence shows no immune deposits. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on electron microscopy of the biopsy specimen?", ["Splitting and thinning of the glomerular basement membrane", "Mesangial IgA deposits", "Effacement of podocyte foot processes", "Subepithelial immune complex humps", "Spike and dome appearance of the basement membrane"], 2, "Minimal change disease is the most common nephrotic syndrome in children and responds well to corticosteroids. Only EM shows the damage: flattened podocyte foot processes. Subepithelial humps are post-streptococcal GN; spike and dome is membranous nephropathy.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "An 8-year-old girl is brought to the physician because her urine has been dark brown, like cola, for 2 days. Her mother noticed that her face looks puffy in the morning. Two weeks ago she had a sore throat with fever that resolved on its own. Blood pressure is 142/94 mm Hg. There is mild swelling of both eyelids and ankles. Urinalysis shows 2+ protein, many red blood cells and red blood cell casts. Serum creatinine is 1.2 mg/dL. Serum C3 concentration is decreased, and antistreptolysin O titer is increased. A kidney biopsy shows hypercellular glomeruli with many neutrophils; immunofluorescence shows granular deposits of IgG and C3. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on electron microscopy?", ["Subepithelial immune complex humps", "Mesangial IgA deposits", "Effacement of podocyte foot processes only", "Linear IgG along the basement membrane", "Splitting of the basement membrane"], 0, "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is a type III hypersensitivity. Immune complexes deposit beneath the epithelium and activate complement, lowering C3. Linear IgG is anti-GBM disease; mesangial IgA is IgA nephropathy, which appears within days of an infection.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 72-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of worsening shortness of breath for 3 days. He has heart failure with a reduced ejection fraction from a previous myocardial infarction. He ate salty food at a family party last weekend. Pulse is 104/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 152/90 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 88% on room air. Jugular veins are distended to the angle of the jaw. There are crackles over both lungs halfway up, and pitting edema of both legs to the knees. He is given intravenous furosemide, and he passes 2 L of urine over the next 3 hours. Which of the following is the site of action of this drug?", ["Aldosterone receptor in the collecting duct", "Carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule", "Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending limb", "Na-Cl cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule", "Epithelial sodium channel in the collecting duct"], 2, "Loop diuretics block NKCC2, removing the gradient that concentrates urine; they are the most powerful diuretics. Side effects include hypokalemia, ototoxicity and loss of calcium. Thiazides act on the DCT; amiloride blocks ENaC; spironolactone blocks the aldosterone receptor.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 46-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of watery diarrhea, about 10 times daily, for 3 days after returning from a trip abroad. He has had mild abdominal cramps but no vomiting or blood in the stool. He takes no medications. Pulse is 108/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. His mucous membranes are dry. Laboratory studies show:\n\nSodium 140 mEq/L\nPotassium 3.1 mEq/L\nChloride 115 mEq/L\nBicarbonate 15 mEq/L\nGlucose 92 mg/dL\n\nArterial blood gas analysis on room air shows a pH of 7.30 and PaCO2 of 30 mm Hg. Which of the following best describes this patient's acid-base disorder?", ["High anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Primary respiratory alkalosis", "Primary respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis"], 1, "Low pH with low HCO3- is metabolic acidosis, with a lower PaCO2 as compensation. Anion gap = 140 - (115 + 15) = 10, which is normal. Diarrhea loses bicarbonate and the kidney keeps chloride instead. DKA, lactic acidosis and methanol raise the anion gap.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A research team is studying kidney blood flow in healthy volunteers. Each volunteer receives a continuous intravenous infusion of a test substance until the plasma concentration is stable. Timed urine samples and plasma samples are then collected. The substance is chosen because it is freely filtered at the glomerulus and almost all of the remaining amount in the peritubular capillaries is secreted by the proximal tubule, so that nearly none remains in renal venous blood. The clearance of this substance is calculated as urine concentration multiplied by urine flow, divided by plasma concentration. Which of the following substances is the team most likely using to estimate effective renal plasma flow?", ["Creatinine", "Inulin", "Glucose", "Para-aminohippuric acid (PAH)", "Urea"], 3, "PAH is filtered and almost completely secreted by the proximal tubule, so nearly all of it is removed in one pass through the kidney. Its clearance approximates renal plasma flow. Inulin is filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals GFR.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 40-year-old man comes to the physician because of dull bilateral flank pain and an episode of red urine for 2 months. He has had high blood pressure for 5 years that has been difficult to control. His mother died of a sudden, severe headache at age 48 years, and his maternal uncle receives dialysis. Blood pressure is 158/98 mm Hg. Large, irregular masses are felt in both flanks. A soft midsystolic click is heard at the apex. Urinalysis shows red blood cells. Serum creatinine is 1.8 mg/dL. Ultrasonography shows enlarged kidneys replaced by many cysts of various sizes, and several cysts in the liver. A mutation in which of the following genes is most likely responsible for this patient's condition?", ["VHL on chromosome 3", "WT1 on chromosome 11", "TSC2 on chromosome 16", "PKD1 on chromosome 16", "PKHD1 on chromosome 6"], 3, "Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is most often due to PKD1 (polycystin-1). It is associated with berry aneurysms, liver cysts and mitral valve prolapse. PKHD1 causes the autosomal recessive childhood form.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Respiratory & renal", "A 60-year-old man comes to the physician because of blood in his urine and a dull ache in his left flank for 1 month. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years and is obese. Temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F) and blood pressure is 148/88 mm Hg. A mass is palpable in the left flank. His face is ruddy. A left-sided scrotal varicocele is present that does not decompress when he lies down. Laboratory studies show a hematocrit of 56% and an increased serum erythropoietin concentration. A CT scan shows a 7-cm mass in the left kidney with tumor extending into the left renal vein. Which of the following best explains this patient's mass and his high hematocrit?", ["Renal angiomyolipoma secreting renin", "Renal cell carcinoma secreting erythropoietin", "Wilms tumor secreting erythropoietin", "Adrenal adenoma secreting cortisol", "Transitional cell carcinoma causing hemoconcentration"], 1, "Renal cell carcinoma comes from proximal tubule cells; smoking is a key risk factor and VHL loss on chromosome 3p is common. It can make EPO, PTHrP, ACTH or renin (paraneoplastic syndromes) and tends to invade the renal vein.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 25-year-old woman comes to the physician because of bloating, frequent loose and foul-smelling stools, and fatigue for 1 year. She has also had an extremely itchy rash on her elbows and knees. She has type 1 diabetes. Her BMI is 18 kg/m2. There are clusters of small blisters and excoriations on the extensor surfaces of the elbows and knees. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 10.1 g/dL and mean corpuscular volume of 74 μm3 with a low ferritin. Upper endoscopy with duodenal biopsy shows flattened villi, deep crypts and many intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which of the following laboratory tests would most strongly support the most likely diagnosis?", ["Anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibody", "IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase antibody", "Perinuclear ANCA", "Anti-mitochondrial antibody", "Fecal calprotectin"], 1, "Celiac disease is an immune reaction to gluten (gliadin) in people with HLA-DQ2 or DQ8. Anti-tTG IgA is the best screening test; check total IgA because IgA deficiency is common in celiac disease. The rash is dermatitis herpetiformis.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 24-year-old man comes to the physician because of crampy pain in the right lower abdomen, watery diarrhea 5 times daily, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss over 4 months. He also has had drainage of pus near his anus. He smokes half a pack of cigarettes daily. Temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F). There is tenderness and fullness in the right lower quadrant. There is an opening beside the anus that drains pus. Hemoglobin is 10.8 g/dL. Colonoscopy shows patchy, deep linear ulcers in the terminal ileum and right colon, separated by normal-looking mucosa, with a cobblestone appearance. The rectum appears normal. Biopsies show inflammation through all layers of the bowel wall and noncaseating granulomas. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Pseudomembranous colitis", "Ulcerative colitis", "Crohn disease", "Celiac disease", "Intestinal tuberculosis"], 2, "Crohn disease can affect any part of the GI tract, most often the terminal ileum. It has skip lesions, transmural inflammation (fistulas, strictures) and noncaseating granulomas. Ulcerative colitis is continuous from the rectum and limited to the mucosa.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 26-year-old nursing student comes to the occupational health clinic for screening before beginning her clinical rotations. She feels well and has no history of jaundice or liver disease. She does not use drugs and has had one sexual partner. She received a series of injections as a child but does not know which ones. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Serum alanine aminotransferase is normal. Hepatitis B studies show:\n\nHBsAg negative\nAnti-HBs positive\nAnti-HBc (total) negative\n\nWhich of the following best explains these results?", ["Immunity from past infection", "Acute infection", "Immunity from vaccination", "Window period of acute infection", "Chronic infection"], 2, "The vaccine contains only HBsAg, so it produces anti-HBs alone. Past infection gives both anti-HBs and anti-HBc. The window period has anti-HBc IgM with neither HBsAg nor anti-HBs.", 1, "F", "Microbiology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 45-year-old man comes to the physician because of burning pain in his upper abdomen for 2 months. The pain wakes him at night and improves when he eats. He takes no medications and does not use NSAIDs. He drinks alcohol occasionally. Vital signs are normal. There is mild epigastric tenderness. Upper endoscopy shows a 1-cm clean-based ulcer in the first part of the duodenum. Biopsies of the gastric antrum show chronic inflammation and curved, rod-shaped organisms on the epithelial surface. A breath test after he swallows urea labeled with carbon-13 shows increased labeled carbon dioxide. Which of the following bacterial enzymes allows this organism to survive in the stomach?", ["Hyaluronidase", "Coagulase", "Beta-lactamase", "Urease", "Catalase"], 3, "Helicobacter pylori breaks urea into ammonia, which neutralizes acid around the bacterium; the urea breath test detects this. H. pylori causes most duodenal ulcers and raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma.", 1, "F", "Microbiology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 48-year-old man comes to the physician because of burning chest pain after meals and when lying down at night for 6 months. He often wakes with a sour taste in his mouth. Antacids help briefly. He is overweight and drinks 3 cups of coffee daily. Vital signs are normal. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. An ECG is normal. Upper endoscopy shows erosions of the distal esophagus without metaplasia. He is started on omeprazole taken 30 minutes before breakfast. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of this drug?", ["Formation of a protective coat over ulcers", "Activation of prostaglandin E1 receptors", "Reversible block of H2 histamine receptors on parietal cells", "Irreversible inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in parietal cells", "Neutralization of stomach acid"], 3, "Proton pump inhibitors block the last step of acid secretion. Long-term use is linked to C. difficile infection, pneumonia, low magnesium and reduced B12 and iron absorption. H2 blockers (famotidine) are reversible; misoprostol is a PGE1 analog.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 22-year-old woman comes to the student health clinic because her roommate noticed her eyes looked yellow this week. She is studying for final exams and has been skipping meals and sleeping little. She feels well otherwise and has no abdominal pain or dark urine. She remembers a similar episode when she had the flu last year. She does not drink alcohol and takes no medications. There is mild scleral icterus. The abdomen is nontender, and there is no hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory studies show a total bilirubin of 3.1 mg/dL, with a direct bilirubin of 0.3 mg/dL. Hemoglobin, reticulocyte count, haptoglobin, lactate dehydrogenase, liver enzymes and alkaline phosphatase are within the reference ranges. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of these findings?", ["Hemolysis from a red cell enzyme defect", "Absent UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity", "Mildly decreased UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity", "Defective secretion of conjugated bilirubin into bile", "Obstruction of the common bile duct"], 2, "Gilbert syndrome is common and harmless; fasting, stress or illness bring out mild unconjugated jaundice. Absent enzyme is Crigler-Najjar type I. Defective excretion of conjugated bilirubin is Dubin-Johnson syndrome.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A male newborn is evaluated in the nursery because he has not passed meconium 48 hours after birth. He was born at term and is breastfeeding poorly; this morning he vomited green fluid. He has upslanting eyes, a flat facial profile and a single palmar crease. His abdomen is distended. The anal canal is in the normal position, and on digital rectal examination the rectum feels tight; as the finger is removed, there is an explosive release of stool and gas. A contrast enema shows a narrow rectum with a dilated colon above it. A rectal suction biopsy shows no ganglion cells in the submucosa. Which of the following is the most likely embryologic cause of this patient's condition?", ["Failure of the midgut to rotate", "Persistence of the vitelline duct", "Failure of the urorectal septum to develop", "Failed migration of neural crest cells into the distal colon", "Incomplete canalization of the duodenum"], 3, "Hirschsprung disease lacks both the Meissner and Auerbach plexuses in the rectum and nearby colon, so that segment cannot relax. It is linked to RET mutations and Down syndrome. A persistent vitelline duct causes Meckel diverticulum.", 1, "F", "Embryology"],
["Gastrointestinal", "A 19-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 6 hours after she swallowed about 40 tablets of an over-the-counter pain reliever after an argument with her boyfriend. She has had nausea and vomited once but otherwise feels well. She takes no other medications. Pulse is 92/min and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. She is alert and anxious. The abdomen is soft with mild tenderness in the right upper quadrant. Serum aminotransferase levels are normal now, but her serum drug concentration plotted on a nomogram is well above the treatment line. Which of the following antidotes should be given now, and how does it work?", ["Flumazenil, which blocks benzodiazepine receptors", "Naloxone, which blocks opioid receptors", "Deferoxamine, which binds iron", "Fomepizole, which inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase", "N-acetylcysteine, which restores glutathione"], 4, "In overdose, more acetaminophen is turned by CYP2E1 into the toxic metabolite NAPQI. Glutathione runs out and liver cells die (centrilobular necrosis). N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione and works best within 8 to 10 hours.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 30-year-old woman comes to the physician because of nervousness, heat intolerance, palpitations and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss over 3 months despite a good appetite. She has been having 3 bowel movements daily. Her menstrual periods have become lighter. Pulse is 112/min and blood pressure is 142/62 mm Hg. Her eyes look prominent, with the upper lids pulled back, and there is swelling of the soft tissue around the eyes. The thyroid gland is diffusely enlarged and nontender, and a bruit is heard over it. There is a fine tremor of the outstretched hands. There are firm, raised plaques on the front of both shins. Serum TSH is undetectable and free T4 is high. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?", ["A TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma", "Antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor", "Antibodies against thyroid peroxidase that destroy the gland", "Viral inflammation of the thyroid", "Autonomous nodules from a TSH receptor mutation"], 1, "Graves disease is a type II hypersensitivity in which antibodies act like TSH. The same antibodies stimulate fibroblasts behind the eye and in the skin, causing exophthalmos and pretibial myxedema.", 1, "F", "Immunology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 50-year-old woman comes to the physician because of fatigue, constipation, cold intolerance and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight gain over 6 months. Her hair has become coarse and her menstrual periods are heavier than before. Her sister has vitiligo. Pulse is 56/min. Her skin is dry and cool. The thyroid gland is diffusely enlarged, firm, rubbery and nontender. Ankle reflexes relax slowly. Serum TSH is 24 μU/mL and free T4 is low. Antibodies against thyroid peroxidase are present at a high titer. A fine-needle aspiration of the thyroid is performed. Which of the following findings is most likely on microscopy?", ["Dense fibrosis extending into neck muscles", "Papillae with Orphan Annie eye nuclei", "Granulomas with multinucleated giant cells", "Lymphocytes forming germinal centers and Hurthle cells", "Tall follicular cells with scalloped colloid"], 3, "Hashimoto thyroiditis is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas and raises the risk of thyroid lymphoma. Granulomas are de Quervain thyroiditis; dense fibrosis is Riedel thyroiditis; Orphan Annie nuclei are papillary carcinoma.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 14-year-old girl with type 1 diabetes is brought to the emergency department because of abdominal pain, vomiting and confusion for 1 day. She stopped taking insulin 3 days ago because she was worried about gaining weight. Pulse is 124/min, respirations are 32/min and deep, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Her breath has a fruity odor and her mucous membranes are dry. Laboratory studies show a glucose of 520 mg/dL, bicarbonate of 9 mEq/L, an anion gap of 26 mEq/L, and a high serum beta-hydroxybutyrate concentration. Increased activity of which of the following enzymes in adipose tissue supplies the substrate for this patient's ketone production?", ["HMG-CoA reductase", "Lipoprotein lipase", "Hormone-sensitive lipase", "Glycogen synthase", "Acetyl-CoA carboxylase"], 2, "Without insulin, hormone-sensitive lipase is no longer suppressed. It releases fatty acids that the liver oxidizes into ketone bodies (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate). Lipoprotein lipase, used to store fat, needs insulin.", 1, "C", "Biochemistry"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 54-year-old man comes for a follow-up visit after a routine screening showed a fasting glucose of 152 mg/dL; a repeat test today shows a hemoglobin A1c of 7.6%. He works as a truck driver, eats mostly fast food and does not exercise. His BMI is 34 kg/m2. Blood pressure is 136/86 mm Hg. There are velvety, darkened patches on the back of his neck. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL. His physician recommends lifestyle changes and starts the first-line oral drug for his condition, explaining that it rarely causes low blood sugar but should be avoided if his kidney function drops sharply. Which of the following is the main mechanism of action of this drug?", ["Decreases glucose production by the liver", "Activates PPAR-gamma in fat and muscle", "Blocks glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule", "Slows carbohydrate absorption in the gut", "Increases insulin release from beta cells"], 0, "Metformin is a biguanide that reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis and improves insulin sensitivity without causing hypoglycemia or weight gain. Its rare serious risk is lactic acidosis, more likely with kidney failure. Sulfonylureas increase insulin release; SGLT2 inhibitors block renal glucose reabsorption.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 38-year-old woman comes to the physician because of weight gain, easy bruising and irregular periods for 1 year. She takes no medications. Blood pressure is 156/96 mm Hg. She has a round, red face, a fat pad on the upper back, and wide purple stretch marks on her abdomen. Her arms and legs are thin, and she has trouble rising from a chair without using her arms. Fasting glucose is 142 mg/dL. Her 24-hour urine cortisol is high, and cortisol is not suppressed after a low-dose overnight dexamethasone test. Her plasma ACTH concentration is high. After a high-dose dexamethasone test, her cortisol falls by more than 50%. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?", ["Ectopic ACTH from a small cell lung cancer", "An ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma", "An adrenal carcinoma", "An adrenal adenoma", "Long-term prednisone treatment"], 1, "High ACTH means the cause is ACTH-dependent. A pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease) still responds partly to feedback, so high-dose dexamethasone suppresses it; ectopic ACTH does not. Adrenal tumors and steroid medicines suppress ACTH.", 1, "C", "Physiology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A newborn is examined in the nursery. She was born at term to a 26-year-old woman after an uncomplicated pregnancy. Examination shows an enlarged clitoris and fused labial folds; a uterus is seen on ultrasonography. Her karyotype is 46,XX. On day 10 of life, she is brought back because of poor feeding, vomiting and lethargy. Pulse is 180/min and blood pressure is 50/28 mm Hg. Her skin is darker than expected. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 124 mEq/L, potassium of 6.9 mEq/L and glucose of 48 mg/dL. Which of the following hormone precursors is most likely to be increased in this patient's serum?", ["Corticosterone", "Cortisol", "11-deoxycorticosterone", "17-hydroxyprogesterone", "Aldosterone"], 3, "21-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common congenital adrenal hyperplasia. Without it the adrenal cannot make aldosterone or cortisol (salt wasting), and the precursors (such as 17-hydroxyprogesterone) are shunted to androgens, virilizing girls. 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency raises 11-deoxycorticosterone and causes hypertension.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 42-year-old man comes to the physician because of muscle weakness and frequent nighttime urination. He has had high blood pressure for 2 years that has not come down despite taking amlodipine, lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide at full doses. He does not drink alcohol or eat black licorice. Blood pressure is 170/100 mm Hg. There is no edema. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 144 mEq/L, potassium of 2.9 mEq/L and bicarbonate of 32 mEq/L. Plasma aldosterone concentration is high and plasma renin activity is low. A CT scan of the abdomen shows a 1.5-cm mass in the left adrenal gland. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Primary hyperaldosteronism", "Cushing syndrome", "Pheochromocytoma", "Liddle syndrome", "Renal artery stenosis"], 0, "An adrenal adenoma or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia makes aldosterone on its own, causing hypertension and hypokalemia; the resulting volume expansion suppresses renin. Renal artery stenosis raises both renin and aldosterone. Liddle syndrome has low renin AND low aldosterone.", 1, "F", "Physiology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 35-year-old woman comes to the physician because of episodes of severe pounding headache, heavy sweating and palpitations, each lasting 10 to 20 minutes, several times a week for 3 months. During an episode in the clinic, her blood pressure is 210/120 mm Hg and pulse is 128/min; between episodes her blood pressure is 148/94 mm Hg. She is pale and has a fine tremor during the episode. Her mother had a thyroid cancer removed at age 40 years. Plasma free metanephrine concentrations are markedly increased. A CT scan shows a 4-cm mass in the right adrenal gland. Surgery is planned. Which of the following drugs should be started first to prepare her for surgery?", ["Propranolol", "Spironolactone", "Phenoxybenzamine", "Lisinopril", "Hydrochlorothiazide"], 2, "In pheochromocytoma, give an irreversible alpha blocker first. Starting a beta blocker alone leaves alpha receptors unopposed, and blood pressure can rise dangerously. A beta blocker can be added later for rapid heart rate.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 26-year-old woman comes to the physician because she has had only 4 menstrual periods in the past year. She also has acne and dark, coarse hair growing on her chin and abdomen. She and her husband have been trying to conceive for 1 year. Her BMI is 33 kg/m2. Blood pressure is 132/84 mm Hg. There are velvety, dark patches in her armpits. Pelvic examination shows no masses. A pregnancy test is negative. Serum LH concentration is increased, with an LH-to-FSH ratio of 3:1. Serum testosterone is mildly increased and prolactin and TSH are normal. Ultrasonography shows enlarged ovaries with more than 20 small follicles around the edge of each. If her condition is not treated, she is at greatest risk of which of the following cancers?", ["Ovarian serous carcinoma", "Choriocarcinoma", "Cervical squamous cell carcinoma", "Breast carcinoma in her 20s", "Endometrial carcinoma"], 4, "In polycystic ovary syndrome, anovulation means no progesterone. The endometrium is exposed to estrogen without progesterone, raising the risk of hyperplasia and carcinoma. PCOS also involves insulin resistance and a high LH-to-FSH ratio.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 20-year-old man comes to the physician because he and his wife have been unable to conceive for 1 year. He reports that he never had much facial hair and he was teased at school for having \"breasts.\" He had learning difficulties in reading. He is 191 cm (6 ft 3 in) tall, with long arms and legs. There is bilateral breast enlargement with glandular tissue under the nipples. Pubic hair is sparse. Both testes are small (2 mL) and firm. Semen analysis shows no sperm. Serum FSH and LH concentrations are high and testosterone concentration is low. Which of the following is the most likely karyotype of this patient?", ["46,XY with androgen receptor mutation", "45,X", "46,XX with SRY translocation", "47,XXY", "47,XYY"], 3, "Klinefelter syndrome: seminiferous tubules are damaged, so inhibin falls and FSH rises; Leydig cell function falls, so testosterone falls and LH rises. Androgen insensitivity (46,XY) gives a female appearance with normal or high testosterone.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician 6 weeks after her last menstrual period because of nausea in the mornings and breast tenderness. A home pregnancy test was positive. Transvaginal ultrasonography shows a single intrauterine gestational sac with a yolk sac and an embryo with cardiac activity. A cystic structure is seen in the right ovary. The physician explains that in early pregnancy, progesterone needed to maintain the uterine lining is still made mainly by the ovary, until the placenta takes over. Which of the following hormones maintains this ovarian progesterone production, and which cells produce it?", ["Luteinizing hormone from the anterior pituitary", "Progesterone from the placenta", "Human chorionic gonadotropin from syncytiotrophoblast", "Human chorionic gonadotropin from cytotrophoblast", "Human placental lactogen from decidual cells"], 2, "hCG acts like LH and supports the corpus luteum until the placenta can make enough progesterone (about weeks 8 to 10). It is the hormone measured in pregnancy tests. Very high hCG suggests a molar pregnancy or choriocarcinoma.", 1, "F", "Embryology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 48-year-old woman comes to the physician after a biopsy of a 3-cm mass in her left breast showed invasive ductal carcinoma. Immunohistochemistry shows that the tumor is negative for estrogen and progesterone receptors and strongly positive (3+) for HER2. One axillary lymph node contains tumor. She has no history of heart disease, and her current left ventricular ejection fraction is 62%. Her treatment plan includes chemotherapy and a monoclonal antibody directed against the HER2 receptor, given for 1 year. Which of the following tests should be monitored regularly during treatment because of a known adverse effect of this antibody?", ["Bone density scan", "Audiometry", "Pulmonary function tests", "Echocardiogram to check left ventricular ejection fraction", "Serum calcium"], 3, "Trastuzumab is a monoclonal antibody against HER2. It can reduce heart function, usually reversibly, so ejection fraction is checked. Doxorubicin also causes cardiotoxicity but it is dose dependent and usually irreversible.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 15-year-old girl is brought to the physician because she has not started having menstrual periods. She is shorter than all of her classmates. As a newborn she had swelling of the hands and feet. She is 140 cm (4 ft 7 in) tall (below the 3rd percentile). She has a low posterior hairline, a webbed neck, a broad chest with widely spaced nipples, and no breast development. Her blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg in the right arm and 104/70 mm Hg in the right leg. Serum FSH and LH concentrations are high. Pelvic ultrasonography shows a small uterus and streak ovaries. Her karyotype is 45,X. Which of the following heart defects is most common in patients with this condition?", ["Transposition of the great arteries", "Ebstein anomaly", "Endocardial cushion defect", "Bicuspid aortic valve", "Tetralogy of Fallot"], 3, "Turner syndrome causes streak ovaries (primary amenorrhea), lymphedema and a horseshoe kidney. The most common cardiac defect is a bicuspid aortic valve; preductal coarctation of the aorta is also common. Endocardial cushion defects are linked to Down syndrome.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Reproductive & endocrine", "A 30-year-old woman comes to the physician because she has not had a menstrual period for 6 months and has noticed milky discharge from both breasts. She has also had headaches and decreased interest in sex. She has not been pregnant and takes no medications. Vital signs are normal. Milky fluid can be expressed from both nipples. Visual fields are full. A urine pregnancy test is negative. Serum prolactin concentration is 210 ng/mL (N < 25). TSH and free T4 are normal. An MRI of the brain shows an 8-mm mass in the anterior pituitary. Which of the following drugs is the most appropriate first-line treatment?", ["Cabergoline", "Octreotide", "Levothyroxine", "Leuprolide", "Haloperidol"], 0, "Prolactinomas usually shrink with dopamine agonists (cabergoline or bromocriptine), because dopamine inhibits prolactin release. Haloperidol blocks dopamine and would raise prolactin. Octreotide treats acromegaly.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Multisystem", "A 74-year-old widower is brought to the physician by his son because of fatigue and bleeding from his gums when he brushes his teeth for 2 months. Since his wife died a year ago, he has eaten mostly tea, toast, crackers and canned soup. He drinks 3 beers daily. He has had several bruises on his legs without injury, and a cut on his hand has not healed after 4 weeks. His gums are swollen, purple and bleed easily. There are small red-purple spots around hair follicles on his thighs, with coiled, corkscrew-shaped hairs. Platelet count, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are normal. Which of the following biochemical reactions is most likely impaired in this patient?", ["Hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues in collagen", "Gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors", "Cross-linking of collagen by lysyl oxidase", "Cleavage of procollagen peptides", "Glycosylation of collagen in the Golgi"], 0, "Scurvy is vitamin C deficiency. Vitamin C is needed by prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases; without it collagen triple helices are unstable and vessels are fragile. Copper is needed for lysyl oxidase; vitamin K for gamma-carboxylation.", 1, "F", "Nutrition"],
["Multisystem", "A 2-year-old girl is brought to a clinic in a famine-affected region because of poor appetite and swelling of her legs for 3 weeks. After her younger brother was born, she was weaned from breastfeeding and has been fed mostly a thin porridge made from cassava. She is irritable and apathetic. Her weight is at the 15th percentile. Her abdomen is protuberant, and the liver edge is palpable 4 cm below the costal margin. There is pitting edema of both legs and feet. Her hair is thin, sparse and reddish, and there are areas of peeling, darkened skin on her legs. Serum albumin is 1.9 g/dL. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", ["Beriberi", "Kwashiorkor", "Rickets", "Marasmus", "Pellagra"], 1, "Kwashiorkor is protein deficiency with some calorie intake. Low albumin causes edema, and too little apolipoprotein makes fat build up in the liver. Marasmus is total calorie deficiency with severe wasting and no edema.", 1, "F", "Nutrition"],
["Multisystem", "A 68-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of fever, chills and confusion for 1 day. She has had burning urination for 4 days. Temperature is 39.7°C (103.5°F), pulse is 122/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 78/42 mm Hg despite 2 L of intravenous fluid. Her skin is warm and flushed. There is tenderness at the right costovertebral angle. Urinalysis shows many leukocytes and nitrites. Blood and urine cultures grow lactose-fermenting, gram-negative rods. Serum lactate is 5.2 mmol/L, and serum TNF-alpha and IL-6 concentrations are markedly increased. Which of the following bacterial components triggers this patient's systemic response, and through which receptor?", ["Teichoic acid through MHC class II", "Capsule polysaccharide through complement receptor 1", "Lipid A of lipopolysaccharide through toll-like receptor 4", "Peptidoglycan through toll-like receptor 9", "Flagellin through CD14 alone"], 2, "Endotoxin (LPS) is in the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria; lipid A is the toxic part. It binds CD14 and TLR4 on macrophages, which release TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-6, causing fever and shock. TLR9 recognizes unmethylated CpG DNA.", 1, "C", "Microbiology"],
["Multisystem", "A 58-year-old woman comes to the physician because of painless lumps in her neck and groin that have slowly grown and shrunk over 2 years. She feels well and has no fever, night sweats or weight loss. Examination shows nontender, rubbery lymph nodes, 1 to 3 cm in size, in the cervical, axillary and inguinal regions. The spleen tip is palpable. Complete blood count is normal. An excisional biopsy of an inguinal node shows crowded, uniform follicles throughout the node that lack tingible-body macrophages. The follicles contain small cells with cleaved nuclei that express CD10 and CD20. Cytogenetic analysis shows t(14;18). Which of the following best describes the effect of this translocation?", ["Overexpresses cyclin D1, which drives the cell cycle", "Overexpresses BCL-2, which blocks apoptosis", "Creates the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase", "Overexpresses c-MYC, which drives the cell cycle", "Inactivates the p53 tumor suppressor"], 1, "Follicular lymphoma moves BCL-2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain promoter. BCL-2 keeps the mitochondrial membrane intact, preventing cytochrome c release and the intrinsic pathway of apoptosis. c-MYC t(8;14) is Burkitt; cyclin D1 t(11;14) is mantle cell lymphoma.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Multisystem", "A genetic counselor evaluates a family with a rare disorder that causes short stature, bowing of the legs and low serum phosphate despite adequate vitamin D intake. The pedigree shows that an affected father has had 3 daughters and 3 sons with an unaffected woman. All 3 daughters are affected, and none of the sons are affected. One affected daughter has had 4 children with an unaffected man: 1 affected son, 1 unaffected son, 1 affected daughter and 1 unaffected daughter. Which of the following is the most likely mode of inheritance of this disorder?", ["Mitochondrial", "X-linked recessive", "X-linked dominant", "Autosomal dominant", "Autosomal recessive"], 2, "A father gives his X chromosome to every daughter and his Y to every son. If one X copy is enough to cause disease, all daughters are affected and no sons. Mitochondrial diseases pass only from the mother. An example is hypophosphatemic rickets.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Multisystem", "A couple who are both from an isolated island population come for genetic counseling before planning a pregnancy. Neither has any symptoms or a family history of disease. On their island, 1 in 10,000 children is born with an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder that causes intellectual disability if not detected early. The population is large, mating is random, and there is no significant migration or new mutation, so it is assumed to be in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. Which of the following is closest to the frequency of carriers of this disorder in the island population?", ["1 in 5,000", "1 in 50", "1 in 100", "1 in 10,000", "1 in 200"], 1, "q squared = 1/10,000, so q = 1/100 and p is about 1. Carrier frequency = 2pq, about 2 x 1/100 = 1/50.", 1, "C", "Genetics"],
["Multisystem", "A 25-year-old Ashkenazi Jewish man comes to the physician because of fatigue, easy bruising and aching pain in his thighs for 1 year. Pulse is 84/min. There is pallor. The spleen is palpable 12 cm below the left costal margin, and the liver is palpable 5 cm below the right costal margin. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 10.2 g/dL and platelet count of 64,000/mm3. An x-ray of the femurs shows flaring of the distal ends in an Erlenmeyer flask shape and areas of thinning. A bone marrow biopsy shows large macrophages with fibrillar cytoplasm that resembles crumpled tissue paper. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?", ["Glucocerebrosidase", "Sphingomyelinase", "Galactocerebrosidase", "Hexosaminidase A", "Alpha-galactosidase A"], 0, "Gaucher disease is the most common lysosomal storage disease and is autosomal recessive. Glucocerebroside builds up in macrophages. Hexosaminidase A is Tay-Sachs; sphingomyelinase is Niemann-Pick; alpha-galactosidase A is Fabry disease.", 1, "F", "Biochemistry"],
["Multisystem", "A 30-year-old woman comes to the physician for genetic counseling after being diagnosed with breast cancer. Her younger brother was treated for an osteosarcoma of the leg at age 16 years and later developed leukemia. Her mother died of a brain tumor at age 40 years, and her maternal grandfather had an adrenal cortical carcinoma in his 30s. Her paternal family history is unremarkable. She has no café-au-lait spots, colon polyps or skin findings. A germline mutation in which of the following genes is most likely in this family?", ["APC", "TP53", "BRCA1", "RB1", "MLH1"], 1, "Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an autosomal dominant loss of p53, the guardian of the genome. It causes many cancers at young ages: sarcomas, breast cancer, leukemia, adrenal and brain tumors. BRCA1 mainly causes breast and ovarian cancer.", 1, "F", "Genetics"],
["Multisystem", "A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue, swelling of his legs and shortness of breath for 4 months. He has noticed that his tongue seems too large for his mouth, and he has had numbness in both hands. Blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg, and he becomes light-headed when he stands. His tongue is enlarged with tooth marks on its edges. There are purple bruises around his eyes. There is pitting edema to the knees. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein. Serum protein electrophoresis shows a monoclonal spike, and urine contains free lambda light chains. Echocardiography shows thick ventricular walls with a sparkling appearance. A fat pad biopsy shows deposits that are apple-green under polarized light after Congo red staining. Which of the following proteins is most likely deposited?", ["Transthyretin", "Beta-2 microglobulin", "Amyloid precursor protein", "Immunoglobulin light chains", "Serum amyloid A"], 3, "AL amyloidosis comes from plasma cell light chains and is linked to myeloma. Serum amyloid A deposits in chronic inflammation (AA amyloidosis); beta-2 microglobulin deposits in long-term dialysis; transthyretin causes senile cardiac amyloidosis.", 1, "F", "Pathology"],
["Multisystem", "In a pharmacology laboratory, healthy volunteers each receive a single dose of one of five substances, and plasma concentrations are measured every hour. For four of the substances, the concentration falls by the same fraction each hour; for example, it halves every 4 hours whether the level is high or low. For one substance, the concentration falls by the same amount each hour, about 15 to 20 mg/dL, until it is almost gone, and when the dose is doubled it takes about twice as long to clear. The students note that this substance has no constant half-life. Which of the following substances most likely shows this elimination pattern?", ["Penicillin", "Digoxin", "Ethanol", "Gentamicin", "Lithium"], 2, "In zero-order elimination the enzymes are saturated, so the same amount is removed per unit time and there is no fixed half-life. Ethanol, phenytoin and high-dose aspirin are the classic examples. Most drugs follow first-order kinetics.", 1, "F", "Pharmacology"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "A company develops a new blood test to screen for colorectal cancer. To evaluate it, investigators test 300 adults who also undergo colonoscopy. Colonoscopy, the gold standard, shows that 100 of the participants have colorectal cancer and 200 do not. The new blood test is positive in 90 of the participants with cancer and in 20 of the participants without cancer. Which of the following is the positive predictive value of the blood test in this study population?", ["About 95%", "90%", "About 18%", "About 82%", "About 10%"], 3, "PPV = true positives / all positives = 90 / (90 + 20) = 90 / 110, about 82%. Sensitivity = 90/100 = 90%, and specificity = 180/200 = 90%. Unlike sensitivity and specificity, PPV changes with disease prevalence.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "A randomized controlled trial evaluates a new anticoagulant for preventing stroke in 4,000 patients with atrial fibrillation. Half of the patients receive the new drug and half receive placebo. All patients are followed for 5 years. A stroke occurs in 20% of the patients in the placebo group and 15% of the patients in the treatment group. The new drug costs $400 per month. Based on these results, how many patients need to be treated with the new drug for 5 years to prevent one stroke?", ["100", "5", "15", "20", "33"], 3, "Absolute risk reduction = 20% - 15% = 5% = 0.05. Number needed to treat = 1 / ARR = 1 / 0.05 = 20. The relative risk reduction (25%) sounds larger but does not give NNT.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "Investigators want to know whether drinking coffee is associated with pancreatic cancer. They identify 200 patients with newly diagnosed pancreatic cancer at 5 hospitals and 400 patients without cancer who were admitted to the same hospitals for other reasons, matched by age and sex. Trained interviewers ask all participants how many cups of coffee they drank per day over the past 10 years. The investigators then compare the proportion of heavy coffee drinkers in the two groups. Which of the following measures of association is most appropriate for this study?", ["Attributable risk", "Incidence rate", "Hazard ratio", "Relative risk", "Odds ratio"], 4, "This is a case-control study: groups are chosen by outcome and exposure is looked at backward. Incidence cannot be calculated, so the odds ratio is used. Cohort studies follow exposure groups forward and use relative risk.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "A pharmaceutical company conducts a randomized controlled trial of a new drug for patients with heart failure. The study uses a significance level of 0.05. The investigators report that 30-day mortality was lower in the drug group than in the placebo group (p = 0.04), and they conclude that the drug reduces mortality. Several years later, three large, well-designed trials and a meta-analysis show that the drug has no effect on mortality. Assuming the later studies are correct, which of the following best describes the error in the original study?", ["Recall bias", "Type I error", "Type II error", "Confounding by indication", "Lead-time bias"], 1, "A type I error (false positive) rejects a null hypothesis that is true; its probability is alpha, usually 0.05. A type II error (false negative) misses a real difference; its probability is beta, and power = 1 - beta.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Biostatistics & epidemiology", "A health system introduces a new low-dose CT screening program for lung cancer in heavy smokers. Before the program, patients were diagnosed when they developed symptoms, at an average age of 68 years, and lived an average of 1 year after diagnosis. After the program, cancers are diagnosed at an average age of 66 years, and patients live an average of 3 years after diagnosis. However, the average age at death from lung cancer is 69 years in both periods, and the lung cancer death rate has not changed. Which of the following best explains the apparent improvement in survival?", ["Lead-time bias", "Observer bias", "Recall bias", "Length-time bias", "Selection bias"], 0, "Lead-time bias: earlier diagnosis adds time to survival measured from diagnosis without delaying death. Use mortality rates to avoid it. Length-time bias happens because screening finds slow-growing tumors more easily.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A 16-year-old girl comes to the physician alone and asks for a prescription for oral contraceptive pills. She has had one male sexual partner for 6 months, and they use condoms most of the time. She has no medical problems, does not smoke, and her blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. She asks the physician not to tell her parents because they would be angry. She lives in a state where minors may consent to contraceptive care. Which of the following is the most appropriate response by the physician?", ["Counsel her and prescribe contraception while keeping the visit confidential", "Call her parents to discuss the request", "Refuse until she is 18", "Prescribe only if she agrees to tell her parents", "Tell her parental consent is required"], 0, "In most US states, minors can consent to contraception, STI care and prenatal care without their parents. Encourage her to involve a trusted adult, but do not require it or break confidentiality.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A 40-year-old man develops heavy bleeding 6 hours after surgery to remove part of his colon. His hemoglobin has fallen from 13 g/dL to 6.8 g/dL, pulse is 124/min and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. He is alert and oriented. The surgeon explains that he needs a blood transfusion and that he may die without it. He is a Jehovah's Witness and says clearly that he understands the risk of death, but he will not accept blood or blood products because of his religious beliefs. He signed an advance directive stating this before surgery. His wife, who is not a Jehovah's Witness, begs the team to transfuse him. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?", ["Ask his family to make the decision", "Transfuse after he loses consciousness", "Obtain a court order to transfuse", "Respect his refusal and use other treatments that he accepts", "Transfuse anyway because his life is in danger"], 3, "An adult with capacity may refuse any treatment, even life-saving treatment. Offer acceptable alternatives (volume expanders, cell saver if accepted). Courts may order transfusion for a child, not for a competent adult.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A 58-year-old woman who recently arrived from Afghanistan comes to the clinic to discuss the results of her breast biopsy, which shows cancer. She speaks only Dari. Her 12-year-old son, who speaks fluent English and Dari, comes with her and offers to translate. A nurse on the unit says she learned some Dari from her grandparents and could help. The clinic has access to telephone and video interpreter services. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to communicate with this patient?", ["Give her written information in English", "Use a staff member who speaks some Dari", "Let her son translate", "Use a trained medical interpreter", "Speak slowly and use simple English"], 3, "Professional medical interpreters are trained to be accurate and keep confidentiality. Family members, especially children, may leave things out or change the meaning, and the role is unfair to a child.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A 54-year-old woman comes to the physician's office to receive the results of a liver biopsy that she had last week after a CT scan showed several masses in her liver. The biopsy shows metastatic adenocarcinoma, most likely from the pancreas. The physician has arranged a quiet private room, turned off her pager, and invited the patient's husband to join at the patient's request. The patient sits down and says, \"I've been so worried all week.\" Which of the following is the most appropriate next step by the physician?", ["State the diagnosis right away in one sentence", "Give her the pathology report to read", "Tell her family first", "Ask the patient what she understands about why the biopsy was done", "Describe all the treatment options"], 3, "The SPIKES method: Setting, Perception (what the patient knows), Invitation (how much she wants to know), Knowledge (share the news in plain words), Emotions (respond with empathy), Strategy and summary.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A second-year resident is reviewing medications for her patients at the end of a busy shift. She realizes that she accidentally entered an order that caused a 72-year-old man to receive twice his usual dose of metoprolol this morning. She examines him: pulse is 62/min and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg, which are similar to his values yesterday, and he feels well. Nobody else has noticed the error, and it is unlikely that he will have any harm. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", ["Not tell the patient because there was no harm", "Wait to see if a complication develops", "Tell the patient what happened and apologize", "Tell only the attending physician", "Change the medical record to show the correct dose"], 2, "Physicians should disclose errors to patients honestly, whether or not harm occurred, and report them through the hospital's safety system. Changing records is never acceptable.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"],
["Communication & ethics", "A 34-year-old man comes to the physician to receive the results of tests ordered after he reported having unprotected sex with several partners during work trips. His HIV antibody and viral load tests are positive. He has been married for 8 years and has unprotected sex with his wife. The physician advises him to tell his wife so she can be tested and protected. He becomes upset, refuses, and says that his wife will leave him if she finds out. His wife is not a patient of this physician. The state has a partner notification program through the health department. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", ["Tell his employer", "Stop treating him until he tells her", "Call his wife right away without telling him", "Encourage him to tell her and explain that the health department may notify her if he does not", "Respect his wish and tell no one"], 3, "Confidentiality can be broken to protect an identifiable third party at serious risk. First counsel and support the patient to disclose. If he still refuses, report to public health, which can notify the partner without naming him.", 1, "F", "Behavioral science"]
);
